// leaves the lamp, at a distance, with something not attached to the lamp; nothing touches, covers,
// wires into or improvises control of a fixture. The 1928 and 1929 makeshifts (Fuchs Appendix E,
// Ridge pp. 27 to 29) and the TM 11-401-2 flash handkerchief are NAMED AS REFUSED in lessons 10 and
// 27, never taught; DI0370's flameproofing recipe is refused in lesson 25. No gear is named by brand.
//
// MARKED LESSONS. Each starts its body with a "> **Before release:**" line naming who must act.
//   QR (qualified reviewer), the brief's eight: 8 (3.2), 9 (3.3), 10 (3.4), 21 (6.3, the dimming
//     paragraph), 22 (7.1, the boundary with overhead hanging and work at height), 25 (8.1),
//     26 (8.2), 27 (8.3). None added: every other lesson keeps lamps, cords and heights out of it.
//   P (practitioner or BAM's demonstration), from brief section 2 (Media; 5.4 "source needed";
//     7.3 "needs a practitioner") and questions 1 and 2:
//     3     BAM's demonstration of reading a real room
//     5     BAM's demonstration of the subject walked toward a window
//     7     BAM's demonstration of matte white paper against a glossy surface
//     8, 9  BAM's demonstration of bounce, and of a dark card beside a white one
//     13    BAM's demonstration of a window as the key
//     15-17 a working lighting practitioner to read the 1969 and 1987 placements against current
