    "For theater volunteers, school and church AV teams, student filmmakers, small broadcast crews and anyone with a phone and a window who wants to light a stage, a studio or a set with what is already there. The course starts by reading the light in a room before touching anything, then works out hard and soft light from three things a lamp maker's research director named in 1916: where the source is, how large it looks from the subject, and how bright the surroundings are. It bounces light off walls and ceilings and counts the whole path, fills a shadow with white paper, takes light away with a dark surface, uses the sun and a window as the key, and places a key, a fill and a back light the way two Army manuals did, including where they disagree. It reads a household bulb's box for its color temperature, looks at what a theater, a television studio and a film set each ask of light, and ends on the safety floor: hot lamps, cords and circuits, and the homemade dimmers and stovepipe spotlights of the 1920s, shown only as what the rules forbid. It is not a qualification or an electrical course, it teaches no one to hang a light, and every lesson that needs a qualified reviewer or a practitioner says so at the top. The third course in The Crew Behind the Show series.",
